***

>From ancient times to our digital present, Strangers and Intimates traces t=
he dramatic emergence of private life, and argues that it is now in mortal =
danger.

In this sweeping history, acclaimed cultural historian Tiffany Jenkins take=
s readers on an epic journey, from the strict separations of public and pri=
vate in ancient Athens to the moral rigidity of the Victorian home, and fro=
m the feminists of the 1970s =E2=80=93 who declared that =E2=80=98the perso=
nal is political=E2=80=99 =E2=80=93 to the boundary-blurring demands of our=
 digital age.

Strangers and Intimates is both a celebration of the private realm and a wa=
rning: as social media, surveillance and the expectations of constant openn=
ess reshape our lives, Jenkins asks a timely question: can private life sur=
vive the demands of the twenty-first century?
